%!PS
% How many machinery entry points a program can CALL, and a digest of which.
%
% The register beside this one counts what a program can reach and WRITE, and
% answers none. That is true and it is not the whole question: an operator is
% not a writable object, so a sealed, read-only object graph still answers
% none while an operator stands that installs a replacement for any part of
% it. Calling such an operator reaches what writing the object would have
% reached, and by a route the writable count cannot see.
%
% So what is counted here is reachability of the entry points themselves,
% from the roots a program actually holds. Those roots are systemdict,
% userdict and globaldict, which need nothing -- and internaldict, which
% needs a password the specification publishes (PLRM 8), so it is a root a
% program holds too, not a place things are hidden.
%
% THERE ARE NO NAMES IN THE OUTPUT, AND THERE MUST NOT BE. A list of the
% entry points a program can still call is a map for whoever would like to
% call them, and this tree and its build logs are published. The count says
% which way the set moved and the digest says whether it moved at all.
% Someone working on it adds a print to the walk locally, where nothing is
% published.
%
% A walk must never NAME what it inspects. A bare name in PostScript executes
% an executable value, so a walk written with bare names runs the machinery it
% was meant to look at; every reference below therefore goes through load.

/M 65521 def
/fold {                      % string . int
  /s exch def /h 1 def
  0 1 s length 1 sub { s exch get h 257 mul add M mod /h exch def } for
  h
} bind def

/stats 4 dict def
/seen 60000 dict def
/hits 4000 dict def

% dotted is the tree's mark for machinery, but it cannot be the whole test:
% a machinery name carrying no dot is exactly the one a census keyed on dots
% would not see. So the two roots are asked different questions -- everything
% in internaldict counts, because internaldict holds nothing else.
/dotted true def

/W {                         % obj depth . -
  6 dict begin
    /dep exch def
    /ob exch def
    dep 0 gt {
      /ob load type /dicttype eq {
        seen /ob load known not {
          seen /ob load true put
          /ob load rcheck not
            { stats /shut stats /shut get 1 add put }
            { /ob load {
                /v exch def
                /k exch def
                /k load type /nametype eq {
                  dotted
                    { /k load 200 string cvs 0 get 46 eq }
                    { true } ifelse
                  { hits /k load true put } if
                } if
                /v load dep 1 sub W
              } forall
            } ifelse
        } if
      } {
        /ob load type /arraytype eq /ob load type /packedarraytype eq or {
          /ob load rcheck {
            seen /ob load known not {
              seen /ob load true put
              /ob load { dep 1 sub W } forall
            } if
          } if
        } if
      } ifelse
    } if
  end
} bind def

/R { /seen 60000 dict def /hits 4000 dict def stats /shut 0 put } bind def
/digest {                    % -  .  int
  /sum 0 def
  hits { pop 200 string cvs fold sum add M mod /sum exch def } forall
  sum
} bind def

% ---- what a program reaches holding no password ----
/dotted true def
R
systemdict 12 W  userdict 12 W  globaldict 12 W
(MACHINERY-CALLABLE-NOPASSWORD ) print hits length =print ( ) print digest ==

% ---- and what the published password adds to that ----
/dotted false def
R
1183615869 internaldict 12 W
(MACHINERY-CALLABLE-PASSWORD ) print hits length =print ( ) print digest ==

% And every name systemdict holds, for the guard to hold against the list
% of operators the specification defines. Counting machinery by the dot it
% is spelled with cannot see a machinery name spelled without one --
% graphicsdict and DEVICE both were, and each answered with live machinery.
% The names go to the guard, which prints only how many are left after the
% subtraction; they are not published here or by it.
(SYSTEMDICT-NAMES-BEGIN) =
systemdict { pop dup type /nametype eq { == }{ pop } ifelse } forall
(SYSTEMDICT-NAMES-END) =

(WALKED) =
quit
